00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:35.400
The silent portfolio killer known as bid-ask spread slippage, especially in ETFs around catalyst events.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:41.240
Picture this scenario. Last week, right after TSMC posted its strong results and raised guidance,

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:47.600
you decided it was time to add to a popular AI-themed ETF. You placed a market order and it

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:54.980
filled at $87.45. At first, you felt good. You got in on the news. But later that evening,

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:00.420
when you checked the tape, you realized the ETF's net asset value had barely moved and the price

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:06.760
you paid was 38 cents above the previous day's closing midpoint. What happened? In the first 18

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:12.380
minutes after the news hit, the bid-ask spread on that ETF widened dramatically. From its usual

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:18.840
tight four cents all the way out to 42 cents. Market makers pulled quotes amid surging retail volume

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:23.280
and genuine uncertainty about how the Iran conflict might ripple through global semiconductor

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:30.120
supply chains. On lower liquidity days, this kind of slippage can easily cost you between 0.4%

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:35.800
and 0.7% on a round-trip trade. For many active Canadian investors, that's enough to completely

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:41.220
erase an entire week of outperformance versus the index. The pro tip most retail investors miss

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:46.700
is to treat every ETF like a unique instrument with its own liquidity fingerprint. Before you ever

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:52.260
click buy, check the 20-day average daily volume and the options open interest. If average daily

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:57.660
dollar volume is below roughly $25 million, professionals immediately switch from market orders

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:02.900
to limit orders placed inside the spread. Platforms like interactive brokers give you excellent

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:08.600
block trading tools and depth of book visibility that make this easy. The biggest misconception is

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:13.940
assuming every liquid ETF trades with the same tightness as a blue chip Canadian bank stock. It

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:19.120
simply isn't true, especially in the first hour after major news or during overlapping geopolitical

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.980
tension. The practical habit I want every listener to adopt is this. Before hitting a market order

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:31.080
around any catalyst event, open your broker's advanced tape, verify real-time spread and depth,

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:37.140
and decide whether a limit order or even waiting 30 to 45 minutes for the spread to normalize makes

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.860
more sense. Small habits like this compound powerfully over years inside a tax advantage to count.

